# 21. Water Conservation Performance Framework

The national baseline supports a six-level performance framework.

| Level               | Core question                                       |
| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Capacity            | Can institutions monitor and manage the resource?   |
| Activity            | What management actions occurred?                   |
| Condition           | What happened to water and habitat?                 |
| Biological response | Did ecosystems respond?                             |
| Adaptation          | Did institutions change when evidence warranted it? |
| Persistence         | Did improvements endure?                            |

This allows the project to avoid a recurring analytical error:

> **Counting management activity as evidence of conservation success.**

---

# 22. Water-Specific Bottlenecks

The existing six-bottleneck framework remains applicable:

### Knowledge

Insufficient information about hydrology, ecology, or pressures.

### Authority

The responsible institution lacks authority over the relevant pressure.

### Capacity

Insufficient personnel, funding, infrastructure, or technical resources.

### Coordination

Multiple institutions act without sufficient integration.

### Ecological

Natural-system constraints limit the response.

### Durability

Short-term improvements cannot be maintained.

Water adds a seventh:

### Allocation

Competing legally or socially recognized demands exceed available water or institutional flexibility.

This distinction will be important.

Allocation conflict is not necessarily an administrative failure.

Sometimes it represents a genuine scarcity problem requiring explicit choices among competing objectives.

